Included at the bottom of /etc/nftables.conf, as `*.nft`.

A file here adds rules to the ruleset the main file builds. To add to a chain
that already exists, name the table and chain WITHOUT a `type`/`hook` line —
that re-opens the chain; repeating the hook declares a second one and nft
refuses the load.

    table inet filter {
        chain input {
            tcp dport 8080 accept
        }
    }

Add to `inet filter` and declare no table of your own. The main file deletes
and rebuilds only that table, so that netavark's and NetworkManager's NAT
tables survive a reload; a table declared here would survive it too, keeping
its old rules alongside the new ones.

50-kdos-services.nft is kdos-powerd's, for System Settings' Firewall page and
`kdos-power firewall`, and is rewritten whole on every toggle: an open service
is its accept rule, and a closed one counts its packets in the named counter
kdos_knock_<name> and leaves them to the chain's drop. Anything you write by
hand belongs in a file of your own, which the daemon never touches; a port
such a file opens is still counted in 50-kdos-services.nft, whose rules come
first.

40-podman.nft ships here and re-opens the forward chain as well as input: it
lets rootful podman's bridge networks (podman0, podman1, ...) route and reach
aardvark-dns.

40-libvirt.nft is installed by the libvirt package and does the same for
libvirt's NAT networks (virbr0, ...): it forwards the guests' traffic and lets
them reach the network's dnsmasq for DHCP and DNS. It goes when libvirt does.

The load is checked before it is applied — see /etc/init.d/25_nftables.sh —
so a file with a syntax error refuses the whole ruleset rather than leaving
half of it applied. The previous ruleset stays in the kernel until a good one
replaces it.
